Per-binary subtype-hook table (new; not an upstream backport). Companion to 0008.

A `ref` of an object hierarchy may hold any subtype the program constructs,
and a subtype may be declared in a module the code holding the `ref` cannot
see (it only has to be imported, and constructed, somewhere). Overwriting or
dropping such a `ref` runs that subtype's lifetime hooks, with no call in the
source. No macro or compile-time view of a type can list its subtypes, so no
analysis that reads source can list the hooks that may run.

This patch makes the compiler say so. The main module's C gains, for an
executable,

    void* nimSubtypeHookTable(NI* count);

which stores the row count in *count and returns a static const array of
    { char const* base; char const* sub; void* hook; NI open; }
For every inheriting object type `sub` whose RTTI the binary emits (every type
an object header is written for, hence every type a `ref` of an ancestor can
hold), and for every proper ancestor `base` of it, there is one row per user
lifetime hook (`=destroy`, `=copy`, `=sink`, `=dup`, `=wasMoved`, `=trace`,
`=deepCopy` written by the user, `sfOverridden`) a value of `sub` may run: its
own, those of every type it holds, and those of every subtype a `ref` or `ptr`
it holds may point to. `hook` is the address of that hook's C function (the
same address Nim code takes of the hook), or NULL in a single row when `sub`
reaches no user hook. `open` is 1 in a row (hook NULL) when `sub` holds a
closure, whose environment's hooks cannot be listed. Names are `module.Type`,
the declaring module's name and the type symbol's name (a `ref object`
declares its object type as `T:ObjectType`). *count is -1 when the memory
manager is not arc/atomicArc/orc, in which case there are no rows.

`defined(nimHasSubtypeHookTable)` announces the accessor. As with 0008 the rows
are static const data, the accessor is a constant function with hidden
visibility (N_LIB_PRIVATE), it is written for the main module of an executable
on the c and cpp backends only (never under --app:lib or --app:staticlib), and
no platform-specific code is involved, so mingw and msvc take the same path.
A consumer must guard on the application type as well as on the define.

Consumer: nelli's global route scan (RFC-0016 PRC-02m) takes the hooks of the
subtypes it reads as those a `ref` of the hierarchy may run, and asks the table
at run time whether any subtype in the binary has another.

Repro: tests/subtype_hook_table/main.nim (a subtype and its hook declared in a
module the main file never imports) and tests/method_dispatch_table_lib/run.sh
(two static libraries define neither accessor).

